{"bfa-ata-extra-widget-area-1":{"custom_html-28":{"title":"","content":"<!-- BEGIN BREADCRUMB (AUTHORS) -->\r\n&#x2937; You are here:&nbsp;&nbsp; <a href=\"\/contributing\/contributors\/\">Contributors (A\u2192Z)<\/a>&nbsp;&nbsp;&#x1FA9C;&nbsp;&nbsp;[wpseo_breadcrumb]\r\n<hr \/>","conditions":{"action":"show","match_all":"0","rules":[{"major":"author","minor":"","has_children":false}]}},"custom_html-2":{"title":"","content":"<!-- BEGIN BREADCRUMB (POSTS) -->\r\n<span style=\"font-size: large;\">&#x2937;<\/span> You are here:&nbsp;&nbsp; [wpseo_breadcrumb]\r\n<hr \/> ","conditions":{"action":"show","match_all":"0","rules":[{"major":"page","minor":"post_type-post","has_children":false}]}},"custom_html-30":{"title":"","content":"<!-- BEGIN BREADCRUMB (PAGES) -->\r\n<span style=\"font-size: large;\">&#x2937;<\/span> You are here:&nbsp;&nbsp; Project Pages&nbsp;&nbsp;&#x27F6;&nbsp;&nbsp; [wpseo_breadcrumb]\r\n<hr \/>"},"custom_html-15":{"title":"","content":"<!-- BEGIN BREADCRUMB (CAT, TAG, AND ARCHIVE PAGES) -->\r\n<span style=\"font-size: large;\">&#x2937;<\/span> You are here:&nbsp;&nbsp; [wpseo_breadcrumb]\r\n<hr \/>","conditions":{"action":"hide","match_all":"0","rules":[{"major":"page","minor":"post_type-post","has_children":false},{"major":"page","minor":"post_type-page","has_children":false},{"major":"author","minor":"","has_children":false},{"major":"page","minor":"404","has_children":false},{"major":"page","minor":"search","has_children":false}]}},"custom_html-17":{"title":"","content":"<div class=\"plain-test-wrapper\">\r\n<!-- THE ESSAY -->\r\n<p \/>\r\n<div style=\"text-align: justify; letter-spacing: 1px; line-height: 1.5em;\"><div style=\"border-left: 6px solid #F39C12; padding-left: 12px;\">Publicly launched in 5769 (2009), the Open Siddur is a volunteer-driven, non-profit, non-commercial, non-denominational, and non-prescriptive community project growing a vast collection of digitized Jewish prayers, liturgies, and related works (historic and contemporary, familiar and obscure), composed in every era, region, and language Jews, adjacent communities, and fellow travelers have ever prayed. For those working with the content of Jewish devotional practice, (e.g., on <span class=\"hebrew\">\u05e1\u05b4\u05d3\u05bc\u05d5\u05bc\u05e8\u05b4\u05d9\u05dd<\/span>, <em>siddurim<\/em> \u2014 prayerbooks), our goal is to provide text, tools, and resources shared under <a style=\"font-weight: bold;\" href=\"https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Free_content\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">open\/libre<\/a> terms for adaptive reuse. Through this effort we hope to empower personal autonomy, to preserve customs, to cross-pollinate wisdom, and to foster openness and vitality in religious culture.<\/div>\r\n<p \/>\r\n\r\n<!-- Expand Button Placeholder (initial position) -->\r\n<div id=\"expand-button-container\" style=\"margin-top: 1em;\">\r\n\t\t<button onclick=\"toggleEssay(this);\" id=\"expand-toggle\" class=\"wp-dark-mode-ignore\" style=\"background: #f39c12; color: #fff; border: none; padding: 8px 16px; font-weight: bold; cursor: pointer; border-radius: 4px;\">\r\n\t\t\tRead more\r\n\t\t<\/button>\r\n<\/div>\r\n\r\n<!-- Hidden Essay -->\r\n<div id=\"essay-extra\" style=\"display: none; margin-top: 1em;\"><div style=\"border-left: 6px solid #F39C12; padding-left: 12px;\">We are a community spanning students studying private and communal Jewish prayer in its literary and historical context, educators preparing curricular resources, authors of new prayers and liturgies, translators of prayers new and old, transcribers of digital text from printed and handwritten works, and ultimately, living practitioners actively producing new siddurim for ourselves and others. We grow and maintain this archive of words, insight, and ritual praxis as a repository for those \"whose hearts have been stirred\" to craft and contribute just as their spiritual forbearers offered their art to the Mishkan (Tabernacle, <span class=\"citation\">cf. Exodus 36:2<\/span>). Everyone engaged in Jewish prayer should always possess the autonomy to craft their own prayer resources \u2014 but do so with careful attention to the attribution, provenance, and lineage of these texts. Thus, almost every resource shared through our project that is not already in the <a href=\"https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Public_domain\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\" target=\"_blank\">Public Domain<\/a> is shared by its creator or copyright steward with a <a href=\"https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Free_content\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\" target=\"_blank\">libre Open Access content license<\/a>. Open Content licenses require that our value of preserving attribution is grounded in the sacred work of editing, arranging, remixing, and redistributing these works \u2014 creative processes where details of authorship, origin, and provenance are easily lost when their importance is not underscored. From the verse \"And Esther said in the name of Mordekhai\" <span class=\"citation\">(Esther 2:22)<\/span> we learn that through attribution, our world is redeemed <span class=\"citation\">(Megillah 15a:20, Pirkei Avot 6:6)<\/span>: the lineage of the source is preserved, the creator is honored, and <a href=\"\/?p=12113\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\" target=\"_blank\">the lattice of kindness supporting the selfless creative act<\/a> is reinforced.<\/div><p \/>\r\n\t\r\n<div style=\"border-left: 6px solid #F39C12; padding-left: 12px;\">Prayers are <em>sacred offerings<\/em>, not <em>chattel property<\/em>. The commodification of prayer texts, historical or contemporary, is anathema to Jewish spiritual practice. The application of restrictive access controls on prayers is an unwelcome and corrupting influence on Jewish religious culture. This project was initiated in opposition to the commodification of the content of Jewish prayerbooks by proprietary interests, whether these be prestige-driven, sectarian\/denominational, private\/commercial, or any other self-interest. We take no issue with the layout, arrangement, and design choices of any author or editor. Rather, it is the underlying unformatted text of a liturgical reading or prayer, in its original language or in translation, that must remain accessible for redistribution and adaptive reuse under 1) <a href=\"https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Open_standard\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\">open technological standards<\/a> that ensure their future-proof reproduction, and 2) <a href=\"https:\/\/wiki.creativecommons.org\/wiki\/best_practices_for_attribution\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\">broadly recognized legal terms<\/a> requiring their correct and proper attribution. If we truly believe that our <em>avodah<\/em> (sacred labor\/work\/craft) is life changing and integral to Jewish life (or even the repair of the world!), then it should remain shareable in every channel available to its distribution, and guarded from monopolization by proprietary interests. In short, public offerings should be shared with open licenses.<\/div><p \/>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"border-left: 6px solid #F39C12; padding-left: 12px;\">Cultures breathe creativity like we breathe oxygen. Our multilingual cultural infrastructure ossifies with the imposition of artificial scarcity; with arbitrary and proprietary barriers to access, our communal diversity devolves into monoculture. Our individual creativity calcifies without the freedom to process the spiritual insights of our deepest selves and our received traditions. Without consideration for personal autonomy, religious tools structured to complement the most profound experiences and relationships can degenerate into instruments of control, inhibiting imagination, vision, and possibility. As personal and communal prayer remains one of the most primary and private experiences of Jewish devotional practice, and since siddurim are the primary means for accessing and engaging in Jewish prayer, we want every practitioner to afford all the resources that would otherwise limit them in compiling their own prayerbook. (One committed at heart to the craft of their own prayerbook should really only be limited by their knowledge of prayers and liturgical customs.) So that Jewish religious culture may continue to grow and thrive, the resources and technologies collected and utilized by this project are shared freely: <em>gratis<\/em> (without cost) and <em>libre<\/em> (without restriction).<\/div><p \/>\r\n\t\r\n<div style=\"border-left: 6px solid #F39C12; padding-left: 12px;\">In an era in which creative engagement in cultural work is mediated by concerns over attribution, authenticity, and integrity, we believe that open-source strategies are key to keeping religious Jewish practice vital, relevant, beautiful, and liberating. Jewish prayers, liturgical customs, and ritual praxes were all developed as part of a cultural <a href=\"https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Commons\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\">commons<\/a>, built collectively over hundreds of generations. We think of siddurim not only as containers of Jewish identity and spiritual heritage, but also as public repositories of effective exercises, prompts, and methods \u2014 shared praxes for growing the creative and emotional intelligence in those utilizing them in their committed practice. And for those who seek a more expansive cultural literacy, an open siddur is a gateway into the diverse customs and languages of the historic and contemporary Jewish world.<\/div><p \/>\r\n\r\n\t<div style=\"border-left: 6px solid #F39C12; padding-left: 12px;\">Our commitment to collaboration and sharing is why we call our siddur project \u201c<span style=\"text-transform: uppercase;\">open<\/span>.\u201d We aspire to be a resource of Jewish prayer as freely shared as the Torah of Loving-kindness (<span class=\"hebrew\">\u05ea\u05bc\u05d5\u05b9\u05e8\u05b8\u05ea\u00a0\u05d7\u05b6\u05e1\u05b6\u05d3<\/span>, as described in <a href=\"https:\/\/www.sefaria.org\/Sukkah.49b.9-13?lang=bi\" target=\"_blank\">Sukkah\u00a049b.9-13<\/a>), transmitted as openly and earnestly as the Torah was shared in the Midbar Sinai, as free a resource for all who enter into the world as fire, water, and wilderness <span class=\"citation\">(cf. <a href=\"https:\/\/www.sefaria.org\/Mekhilta_DeRabbi_Yishmael%2C_Tractate_Bachodesh.5.11?lang=bi&with=all&lang2=en\" target=\"_blank\">Mekhilta d'Rebbi Yishmael \u00a7 Ba\u1e25odesh 5.11<\/a>)<\/span>. In observing this principle, we align our project values with <a href=\"http:\/\/opendefinition.org\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\">the definition of open content and open data<\/a> maintained by the <a href=\"https:\/\/okfn.org\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Open Knowledge Foundation<\/a>, the <a href=\"https:\/\/opensource.org\/osd-annotated\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\">definition of open-source<\/a> maintained by the <a href=\"https:\/\/opensource.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Open Source Initiative<\/a>, and <a href=\"http:\/\/freedomdefined.org\/definition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\">the four values of libre\/free culture<\/a> maintained by the <a href=\"http:\/\/freeculture.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\">Free Culture Foundation<\/a>.<\/div><p \/>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"border-left: 6px solid #F39C12; padding-left: 12px;\">Have a favorite prayer, piyyut, or praxis? If it's not yet in the archive, then <a href=\"\/contribute\/upload\/\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\" target=\"_blank\">please upload it<\/a>! Make it available to others studying Jewish prayer or crafting their own prayerbooks.<\/div><p \/>\r\n\r\n\t<div style=\"text-align: right;\">Read more about our <a href=\"\/development\/mission\/\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\" target=\"_blank\">mission<\/a> and <a href=\"\/development\/history\/\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\" target=\"_blank\">project history<\/a>, and <a href=\"\/policies\/copyright-policy\/\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\" target=\"_blank\">copyright policy<\/a>. If your question isn't answered there, please <a href=\"\/contact\/\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\" target=\"_blank\">contact us<\/a>. \u261c<\/div><\/div>\r\n\r\n\t\t<div id=\"expand-button-bottom\"><\/div>\r\n<\/div>\r\n<!-- END THE ESSAY -->\r\n<\/div>\r\n\r\n<script>\r\n  function toggleEssay(btn) {\r\n    const essay = document.getElementById('essay-extra');\r\n    const isHidden = essay.style.display === 'none';\r\n    const topContainer = document.getElementById('expand-button-container');\r\n    const bottomContainer = document.getElementById('expand-button-bottom');\r\n\r\n    if (!isHidden) {\r\n      \/\/ Save scroll position BEFORE collapsing\r\n      const scrollY = window.scrollY;\r\n      \r\n      \/\/ Collapse content\r\n      essay.style.display = 'none';\r\n      btn.textContent = 'Read more';\r\n      topContainer.appendChild(btn);\r\n\r\n      \/\/ Restore scroll position to prevent jump\r\n      window.scrollTo({ top: scrollY });\r\n    } else {\r\n      \/\/ Expand content\r\n      essay.style.display = 'block';\r\n      btn.textContent = 'Close';\r\n      bottomContainer.appendChild(btn);\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n<\/script>\r\n\r\n[spacer height=\"30px\"]\r\n<hr \/>"},"custom_html-37":{"title":"Project Activity & Recent Contributors","content":"<div class=\"plain-text-wrapper\">The project activity \"heatmap\" below displays a calendrical grid of resources published through the project over the course of each year since the inauguration of this project website. Alongside is a display of project contributors (authors and translators), who have contributed through the project in the last 30 days. (For a full index of project contributors, <a href=\"\/contributors\/\">go here<\/a>.) The view expressed by any one individual contributor represents their own point-of-view and reflects the plurality of voices expressed through this project. Any individual viewpoint does not necessarily represent that of any other volunteer contributor, the project's developers, or any of its institutional partners. Contributors whose portraits are unavailable are displayed as a tree in a field, per Deuteronomy 20:19.<\/div>\r\n[spacer height=\"20px\"]\r\n<div class=\"shortcode-grid\">\r\n\t\t<div class=\"shortcode-item\">\r\n\t\t\t[contributor_activity_heatmap]\r\n\t\t<\/div>\r\n\t\t<div class=\"shortcode-item\">\r\n\t\t\t[featured_contributors status=\"active\" min_posts=\"1\" weeks=\"4\" omit=\"131,820,2241\"]\r\n\t\t<\/div>\r\n<\/div>\r\n\r\n[spacer height=\"30px\"]","conditions":{"action":"show","match_all":"0","rules":[{"major":"page","minor":"front","has_children":false}]}},"rpwe_widget-7":{"title":"Recently Added Prayers, Songs, and Other Resources","title_url":"","ignore_sticky":true,"exclude_current":0,"limit":7,"offset":0,"order":"DESC","orderby":"date","post_type":["post"],"post_status":"publish","cat":null,"tag":null,"taxonomy":"category=1132,230,3145,1295,97,2099","excerpt":false,"length":10,"date":false,"date_relative":true,"date_modified":true,"readmore":false,"readmore_text":"Read More \u00bb","comment_count":false,"thumb":true,"thumb_height":75,"thumb_width":75,"thumb_default":"https:\/\/opensiddur.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2017\/05\/judenhat-50x50-2.png","thumb_align":"rpwe-alignleft","styles_default":true,"cssID":"","css_class":"","css":".rpwe-block ul{\r\nlist-style: none !important;\r\nmargin-left: 0 !important;\r\npadding-left: 0 !important;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-block li{\r\nborder-bottom: 1px solid #eee;\r\nmargin-bottom: 10px;\r\npadding-bottom: 10px;\r\nlist-style-type: none;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-block a{\r\ndisplay: inline !important;\r\ntext-decoration: none;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-block h3{\r\nbackground: none !important;\r\nclear: none;\r\nmargin-bottom: 0 !important;\r\nmargin-top: 0 !important;\r\nfont-weight: 400;\r\nfont-size: 1.2em !important;\r\nline-height: 1.5em;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-thumb{\r\nborder: 1px solid #eee !important;\r\nbox-shadow: none !important;\r\nmargin: 2px 10px 2px 0;\r\npadding: 3px !important;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-summary{\r\nfont-size: 12px;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-time{\r\ncolor: #111;\r\nfont-size: 1em;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-comment{\r\ncolor: #bbb;\r\nfont-size: 11px;\r\npadding-left: 5px;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-alignleft{\r\ndisplay: inline;\r\nfloat: left;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-alignright{\r\ndisplay: inline;\r\nfloat: right;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-aligncenter{\r\ndisplay: block;\r\nmargin-left: auto;\r\nmargin-right: auto;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-clearfix:before,\r\n.rpwe-clearfix:after{\r\ncontent: \"\";\r\ndisplay: table !important;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-clearfix:after{\r\nclear: both;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.rpwe-clearfix{\r\nzoom: 1;\r\n}\r\n","before":"<div class=\"plain-text-wrapper\" style=\"margin-bottom: 40px;\">Below are the seven most recent resources shared by project contributors.<\/div>","after":"","conditions":{"action":"show","match_all":"0","rules":[{"major":"page","minor":"front","has_children":false}]},"post_title":true,"link_target":false,"css_id":""},"custom_html-43":{"title":"","content":"[spacer height=\"30px\"]","conditions":{"action":"show","match_all":"0","rules":[{"major":"page","minor":"front","has_children":false}]}},"custom_html-40":{"title":"Main Categories","content":"[custom_category_grid term_ids=\"1132,230,2099,91\" svg_width=\"325\" image_percentage=\"85\" image_size=\"medium\" gap=\"20\"]\r\n\r\n[spacer height=\"30px\"]","conditions":{"action":"show","match_all":"0","rules":[{"major":"page","minor":"front","has_children":false}]}},"custom_html-39":{"title":"Upcoming Festivals, Feasts & Fasts","content":"[spacer height=\"20px\"]\r\n\r\n[custom_category_grid term_ids=\"2650,576,410,4533,1080,3397\" svg_width=\"250\" image_percentage=\"85\" image_size=\"medium\" gap=\"10\"]\r\n\r\n[spacer height=\"30px\"]"},"custom_html-41":{"title":"Upcoming Civic Days on Civil Calendars","content":"[spacer height=\"20px\"]\r\n\r\n[custom_category_grid term_ids=\"1538,3721,2073,3287,6629\" svg_width=\"250\" image_percentage=\"85\" image_size=\"medium\" gap=\"10\"]\r\n\r\n[spacer height=\"30px\"]"},"custom_html-42":{"title":"Select Additional Categories","content":"[spacer height=\"20px\"]\r\n\r\n[custom_category_grid term_ids=\"188,384,1127,4505,234,802,426,2638,4588,782,781,1106,1001,1257,2558,2554,5362,2686,5513\" svg_width=\"250\" image_percentage=\"85\" image_size=\"medium\" gap=\"10\"]\r\n\r\n[spacer height=\"30px\"]\r\n\r\n<a href=\"#\" class=\"nks_cc_trigger_element\" style=\"font-weight: 900 !important;\">More categories...<\/a>\r\n[spacer height=\"50px\"]\r\n<hr \/>","conditions":{"action":"show","match_all":"0","rules":[{"major":"page","minor":"front","has_children":false}]}},"custom_html-38":{"title":"\u05d6\u05db\u05e8 \u05dc\u05d1\u05e8\u05db\u05d4 | May their names be remembered for a blessing","content":"<div class=\"plain-text-wrapper\">\"<em>Zekher liVrakhah<\/em>\" (lit. \"remembered for a blessing,\" Cf. Proverbs 10:7). Displayed below are a selection of authors and translators of previous generations to whom resources shared through this project have been  credited and attributed. Contributors whose portraits are unavailable are displayed as a tree in a field, per Deuteronomy 20:19.<\/div>\r\n[spacer height=\"20px\"]\r\n<div class=\"featured-contributors-wrapper\">[featured_contributors status=\"inactive\" limit=\"18\" min_posts=\"5\" weeks=\"0\"  omit=\"1630,328,470,295,1305,1629,310,509,511,513,508,507,344,696,533,684,691,1094,353,1998\"]<\/div>","conditions":{"action":"show","match_all":"0","rules":[{"major":"page","minor":"front","has_children":false}]}}},"bfa-ata-extra-widget-area-2":{"custom_html-13":{"title":"","content":"<!-- BEGIN PAGE HEADER -->\r\n<div class=\"wrap\">\r\n\t\t<a href=\"\/\">\r\n\t\t\t<img src=\"https:\/\/opensiddur.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/09\/Open-Siddur-Project-Logo-2016.svg\" alt=\"the Open Siddur Project [logo]\" width=\"115\" height=\"115\" class=\"skip-lazy\" \/>\r\n\t\t<\/a>\r\n\r\n\t\t<div class=\"text-block\">\r\n\t\t\t<div class=\"site-name\">\r\n\t\t\t\t<a style=\"text-decoration: none; color: inherit; line-height: 1.5em;\" href=\"\/\">\r\n\t\t\t\t\t<span style=\"font-size:x-large;\">\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\tthe Open Siddur Project \r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<span style=\"vertical-align: middle;\">&#x270D;&#xFE0E;<\/span>&nbsp;\u05e4\u05bc\u05b0\u05e8\u05d5\u05b9\u05d9\u05b6\u05e7\u05b0\u05d8&nbsp;\u05d4\u05b7\u05e1\u05bc\u05b4\u05d3\u05bc\u05d5\u05bc\u05e8&nbsp;\u05d4\u05b7\u05e4\u05bc\u05b8\u05ea\u05d5\u05bc\u05d7\u05b7\r\n\t\t\t\t\t<\/span>\r\n\t\t\t\t<\/a>\r\n\t\t\t<\/div>\r\n\r\n\t\t\t<div class=\"tagline\">\r\n\t\t\t\t<span style=\"letter-spacing: 1px;\">\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta community-grown, libre Open Access archive of Jewish prayer and liturgical resources \u2014 for those crafting their own prayerbooks and sharing the content of their practice\r\n\t\t\t\t<\/span>\r\n\t\t\t<\/div>\r\n\t\t<\/div>\r\n<\/div>\r\n<!-- END PAGE HEADER -->"}},"nks_area_1":{"custom_html-25":{"title":"","content":"<a href=\"\/\">the Open Siddur Project<\/a>&nbsp;&nbsp;\/\/&nbsp;&nbsp;Main Menu"},"ajaxsearchprowidget-2":{"title":"","searchid":"3"},"custom_html-45":{"title":"","content":"&nbsp;<br \/><p \/>\r\n\r\nCategories Index:<p \/>\r\n\r\n[iks_menu id=\"40696\"]\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n[iks_menu id=\"60926\"]\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n[iks_menu id=\"28934\"]\r\n"},"custom_html-46":{"title":"","content":"<style>\r\n.si-list {\r\n    \/* Easy to retune later - only these three colors control the whole look *\/\r\n    --si-bg: #DD9933;        \/* circle background (matches your current orange) *\/\r\n    --si-hover-bg: #1e73be;  \/* hover color *\/\r\n    --si-icon-color: #ffffff;\r\n\r\n    display: flex;\r\n    flex-direction: column;\r\n    gap: 10px;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.si-item__link {\r\n    display: flex;\r\n    align-items: center;\r\n    gap: 12px;\r\n    text-decoration: none;\r\n    color: inherit;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.si-item__icon {\r\n    flex: 0 0 auto;\r\n    width: 36px;\r\n    height: 36px;\r\n    border-radius: 50%;\r\n    background: var(--si-bg);\r\n    color: var(--si-icon-color);\r\n    display: flex;\r\n    align-items: center;\r\n    justify-content: center;\r\n    transition: background-color 0.2s ease;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.si-item__icon svg {\r\n    width: 18px;\r\n    height: 18px;\r\n    fill: currentColor;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.si-item__link:hover .si-item__icon,\r\n.si-item__link:focus .si-item__icon {\r\n    background: var(--si-hover-bg);\r\n}\r\n\r\n.si-item__label {\r\n    font-size: 0.95em;\r\n}\r\n<\/style>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"si-list\">\r\n\r\n    <div class=\"si-item\">\r\n        <a class=\"si-item__link\" href=\"https:\/\/opensiddur.org\/feed\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener me\" title=\"RSS Feed\">\r\n            <span class=\"si-item__icon\">\r\n                <svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><circle cx=\"6.18\" cy=\"17.82\" r=\"2.18\"\/><path d=\"M4 4.44v2.83c7.03 0 12.73 5.7 12.73 12.73h2.83c0-8.59-6.97-15.56-15.56-15.56zm0 5.66v2.83c3.9 0 7.07 3.17 7.07 7.07h2.83c0-5.47-4.43-9.9-9.9-9.9z\"\/><\/svg>\r\n            <\/span>\r\n            <span class=\"si-item__label\">RSS Feed (rss)<\/span>\r\n        <\/a>\r\n    <\/div>\r\n\r\n    <div class=\"si-item\">\r\n        <a class=\"si-item__link\" href=\"https:\/\/opensiddur.org\/help\/rss-to-email-subscriptions\/\" rel=\"noopener me\" title=\"Weekly Digest Email\">\r\n            <span class=\"si-item__icon\">\r\n                <svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M2 4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h16a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v16a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4zm2 .4v.6l8 6 8-6v-.6H4zm16 3.03-7.4 5.55a1 1 0 0 1-1.2 0L4 7.43V20h16V7.43z\"\/><\/svg>\r\n            <\/span>\r\n            <span class=\"si-item__label\">Weekly Digest (email)<\/span>\r\n        <\/a>\r\n    <\/div>\r\n\r\n    <div class=\"si-item\">\r\n        <a class=\"s